## Tests

* `<tests>` holds one or more `<test name="...">` entries. Each test's `name` attribute is the literal test name/description the plan prompt and the eventual test suite will use — pick names precise enough that "does a test with this name exist and pass" is a mechanical check, not a judgment call (`authenticate_rejects_expired_token`, not `test_auth`). The element's text content is what passing that test demonstrates, in the reader's terms: reconciliation checks that the named test exists and passes, not that this prose is honored, so the name and the text are what a human is trusting when they read `<tests>` instead of the generated suite itself.
* At least one `<test>` is required, and the list should be proportionate to the behavior it's certifying: cover the primary paths named in `<behavior>`, the properties named in `<invariants>`, and — at minimum — every condition `<edge-cases>` names. A `<tests>` block with fewer named tests than `<edge-cases>` has distinct conditions is under-specified; the plan prompt and the reviewer will both read that gap as a section that didn't finish its job.

## References

* `<references>` is optional — omit it entirely when nothing is attached. When present, it lists `<reference target="ref_..."/>` entries for every attached ref (see the attached-references input above); each target must be one of the refs actually attached to this leaf.

## Style

* Prose, not pseudocode. The plan prompt is a strong reader; it will translate your prose into code structure. Don't try to pre-write code here — that's the plan's job and your code would clash with the project's conventions.
* Name specific types, specific operations, specific failure modes when they're project-distinctive. Avoid generic language that could describe any module.
* Don't restate the parent subcomponent's public surface — the plan prompt reads that separately. Focus on what this leaf *internally* does and guarantees.
* Unescaped `&` and `<` in prose are fine — the parser tolerates them.

## Implementation scope

* You are describing one leaf. Don't describe sibling leaves or the parent component's other slices. If you need to reference them, name the dependency's public surface and leave the internals to them.
* If the leaf's behavior spans multiple entry points, cover all of them in `<behavior>` — don't treat one as primary and the others as footnotes.
* Keep it proportionate to the leaf's complexity. A simple cache wrapper doesn't need 2000 words; a session-management engine does. Pad isn't helpful; underspecifying is worse.

## Phasing

<!-- voided: phase machinery dropped, v5 §6 ("Dropped from v4: the phase machinery... the phase_plan projection and plan rule"). Siege's per-phase authoring rules — related_features_summary as a phase-closure, prior_phase_impl_body delta-style authoring, dep_fanin_summaries, phase-≤N stubbing of the rest of the surface — have no v5 analogue; siege's own §Phasing rules do not carry forward. -->

This tier is `scope: per(subcomp)`, not v4's `per(subcomp) × phase`: one implementation document per subcomponent, authored once, describing its **total end state** rather than one phase's slice of it. There is no prior-phase draft to author delta-style against, no responsibility closure narrower than the subcomponent's whole scope, and no stub-the-rest-of-the-surface discipline — cover the subcomponent's full public surface as declared by its subcomparch, in one pass.
